    "description": "Rehabilitation helps people improve functioning and participation after illness or injury, or while living with a lasting health condition. Iranian research identifies access, coordination, financing, and integration with primary care as important challenges for these services.",
    "tags": ["Rehabilitation", "Disability", "Physiotherapy", "Accessibility", "Primary care", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:03:08Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:03:08Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Rehabilitation Centers in Iran: Services and Access",
      "description": "Explore rehabilitation’s role in everyday functioning and the evidence on access, coordination, and primary-care integration in Iran."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "purpose",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Care directed toward everyday life",
        "content": "Rehabilitation focuses on what a person can do in daily life and how their surroundings affect that ability. The World Health Organization describes an approach combining treatment, assistive products, adaptations to tasks or environments, and support for self-management. Goals can include communicating after a brain injury, moving more independently, or returning to family and working life. Rehabilitation may complement surgery or medical treatment; it is not restricted to people with permanent disabilities. [s1]\n\nA rehabilitation center is therefore better understood through its services and team than through its name alone. WHO identifies professions including physiotherapy, occupational therapy, speech and language therapy, audiology, prosthetics and orthotics, rehabilitation nursing, and physical medicine and rehabilitation. Care may take place in hospitals, outpatient practices, homes, schools, or workplaces. These are general service possibilities, not a claim that every Iranian center offers the full range. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access involves more than a building",
        "content": "Iranian qualitative research published in 2015 explored the experiences of people with disabilities, families, providers, policymakers, and nongovernmental participants. It identified barriers involving service organization, education, identification and screening, stewardship, sociocultural factors, accessibility, and finances. This broadens the meaning of access: a service can exist while remaining difficult to discover, reach, coordinate, or afford. [s2]\n\nParticipants also raised concerns about teamwork and fragmented responsibilities. Such findings explain why the presence of individual specialists does not by itself establish a coordinated rehabilitation pathway. However, interview evidence describes experiences and perceived mechanisms; it cannot determine how frequently every barrier occurs across the country. Nor should a historical study be treated as proof that all the same arrangements remain unchanged. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "integration",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connecting rehabilitation with primary care",
        "content": "A 2022 Iranian policy study examined options for bringing rehabilitation more effectively into primary health care—the first-contact services through which people enter the health system. Its proposals included better referral pathways, improved professional awareness, mobile rehabilitation teams, telerehabilitation, and the use of local volunteers. These suggestions address continuity between identifying a functional difficulty and receiving appropriate support. [s3]\n\nThe same study treated financing, workforce development, information systems, and access to equipment as connected policy issues. It proposed stronger public funding, defined service packages, improved payment arrangements, expanded workforce capacity, and better information for planning. These are policy options derived from participants’ perspectives. The article does not establish that they were subsequently implemented nationwide or that they produced measured improvements by the editorial cutoff. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assessment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding service quality",
        "content": "WHO’s person-centered approach makes an individual’s goals and preferences central to rehabilitation. This means that the relevant outcome extends beyond attendance at a clinic: it concerns functioning and participation in the person’s own environment. [s1] Read alongside the Iranian access studies, that principle suggests a useful editorial distinction between available appointments and sustained, coordinated care. Current local service availability, financial coverage, and outcomes still require direct verification before this page can describe particular centers or compare regions.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
